...
if (some_vertext_attr != 0)
{
    do this
}
else
{
    do that
}
...

```

I don't see how any of these two cases can be stripped of "dead code" at compile time really. 

In fact the second case with dynamic branching based on vertex attr value is even worse than the first case based on uniform as the branching may take different paths for every single fragment thread in the warp/wavefront group. In which case I believe the GPU will always compute both branches for every thread in the group discarding the result of the false path in the end. Essentially double work.

You are missing the point. _Both_ branches are compiled out. The compiler doesn't look at each line in isolation and try to guess what the program will do. It starts from frag_color and works backwards, only including the code that contributes to frag color. Those uniform branches _dont_ contribute to frag_color so the compiler doesn't even touch them, they never get processed and they don't make it into the final binary. 

You are mistakenly thinking the compiler runs through each line and tries to decide if it can be optimized out. That's not how DCE works. 

Consider this example:

```
int foo(int x, int y, int z) {
   int val = 0;
   for (int i = 0; i < x; i++) {
      // Can be any expensive function
      val += log(sin(x) * cos(y));
   }
   return z;
}
```

The compiler doesn't know what `x` or `y` will be. But it really doesn't matter since it will always return z. Its the same with the unshaded shader. The compiler doesn't know what the value of the uniforms will be, so it doesn't know if the branch will be taken or not. But since the branch doesn't change the output value _it doesn't matter_ whether the branch will be taken or not. In the final binary, only the code that contributes to the output value will be included. In this case the final binary will produce identical code to:

```
int foo(int z) {
   return z;
}
```

That's just how DCE works. There is no need to worry about the complexity of `x` or `y` or the logic in the for loop. It all gets eliminated

You really need to take a look at a profiler to understand this if you aren't willing to take my word for it. The profiler will make it immediately clear. 

At any rate. There isn't a problem with Godot, this is just something that you haven't learned/understood yet. So I'll go ahead and close this report. 

thank you for your concern. But this is a knowledge gap you need to close, not a big in Godot.
